Topic 2. Intrusion Prevention 3.2 

                                                IDS vs IPS — The Core Split
          IDS (Intrusion Detection System)	                                        IPS (Intrusion Prevention System)
Role	    Detects and alerts                        	                                      Detects and blocks
Mode  	  Passive / detective	Active
Placement	              Out-of-band — sees a copy of traffic via SPAN port or network TAP	      Inline — traffic physically flows through it
Can it stop an attack?  	No — the malicious packets still reach their destination	            Yes — drops packets, resets connections, blocks IPs in real time
If it crashes	No impact on traffic flow (you just lose visibility)                            	Can interrupt the traffic path entirely
False positive cost	An alert someone has to read	                                              A legitimate connection gets blocked

Exam rule of thumb: if the question says "monitor without affecting traffic" → IDS. If it says "stop/block/prevent" → IPS. 

Deployment Topologies
Inline (IPS): Internet → Firewall → IPS → Core Switch → Internal network. The IPS is in the path; it inspects every packet and can drop it.
Tap / SPAN (IDS): The switch sends a mirror copy of traffic to the IDS. The IDS watches but is not in the data path — so it can never cause downtime, but also can never block. 
Professor Messer makes the point that you can technically run an IPS in tap mode (it sees a copy), but then it behaves like an IDS because it can't act on the traffic.

One small exam-language nudge: the precise terms the exam wants you to use are inline (IPS) vs out-of-band or passive (IDS, fed by a TAP or SPAN port). "Before the switch"
is fine in plain English, but if a question asks for the placement mode, "inline" is the keyword that earns the mark.


Network-Based vs Host-Based
                                  Network (NIDS/NIPS)	                                    Host (HIDS/HIPS)
Scope	                      Monitors traffic across a network segment              	Monitors activity on a single endpoint
Typical placement	        At trust boundaries (edge, DMZ)	                          On servers, workstations
What it sees	              Packets flowing through the wire	                      System calls, file changes, process behaviour, logins

Detection Methods
   Method	                                  How it works	                        Strength	                                                      Weakness
Signature-based	        Matches traffic against a database of known attack patterns (like an antivirus signature)	        High accuracy, low false positives for known threats	    Misses zero-days / novel attacks
Anomaly-based	          Builds a baseline of "normal" traffic, flags deviations	Can catch zero-days and unknown threats    	Higher false-positive rate (what's "normal" shifts)

Most real-world deployments use both — signatures for the known stuff, anomaly detection as a safety net.

Failure Modes (exam favourite)
Fail-open: if the IPS crashes, traffic flows uninspected. Availability is preserved, security is not. 
Fail-closed: if the IPS crashes, traffic is blocked. Security is preserved, availability is not.
The exam will ask you which mode to choose in a given scenario. A hospital or financial trading floor might prefer fail-open (can't afford downtime); a high-security military network might prefer fail-closed.

Where It Sits in the Stack
NGFW (Next-Gen Firewall) often has IPS built in — it does stateful inspection plus deep packet inspection plus app awareness, so it's a superset.
WAF (Web Application Firewall) is a specialised IPS for HTTP/HTTPS traffic (SQLi, XSS, CSRF). 
SIEM ingests the alerts from IDS/IPS and correlates them across the environment.

Analogy -  Quick Mental Model (Mutley/Alan the fool framing)
Mutley at the gate (IPS, inline): checks every person's ticket with his special ink before letting them in. If the ticket is bad, he physically blocks them. If Mutley gets
sick (crashes), either the gate stays open (fail-open) or it stays shut (fail-closed). The security camera on the wall (IDS, tap): watches everyone walk through the gate
and calls the office if someone looks suspicious. But it can't stop them — they're already through the door.
Alan (the target): the person walking through the gate, hopefully with a valid ticket.

QUESTIONS:
An IPS in your DMZ goes down during a major sales event. Your business says "we cannot lose a single transaction." 
Should it have been configured fail-open or fail-closed? Why?

Fail-open → traffic flows uninspected, transactions complete, availability preserved ✓
Fail-closed → traffic blocked, transactions lost, availability sacrificed ✓
One thing to keep in your back pocket for the exam: the trade-off sentence. Fail-open preserves availability but sacrifices confidentiality/integrity during the outage
— the malicious traffic that would have been blocked is now getting through unimpeded. If a question asks "what's the risk of fail-open?" that's the answer they want.

You're deploying protection for a web application that's getting hit with SQL injection and XSS attempts. What type of device do you reach for, and where does it
sit relative to the web server?

WAF (Web Application Firewall), not just a generic IPS

IPS → inspects packets at layers 3–4 (and sometimes 7), catches things like port scans, known exploit signatures, malformed packets.
WAF → a specialised IPS that operates at layer 7 (application layer), specifically for HTTP/HTTPS. It understands SQL syntax, HTML, JSON, etc. 
That's what lets it catch SQLi payloads (' OR 1=1 --) and XSS strings (<script>alert(1)</script>) that a generic IPS might miss because it's not parsing
the application protocol. So the exam answer is: WAF, placed in the DMZ, inline between the firewall and the web server. It's a WAF because the threat is 
application-layer (SQLi, XSS), not just network-layer.

If a question said "port scan" or "SYN flood" → IPS. If it said "SQL injection" or "XSS" → WAF.
An extra through in regards to Nmap and Metasploit kali tools for hacking 
Nmap scan → NIPS (Network IPS)

Nmap's scan patterns are a classic L3/L4 signature. The IPS has rules for:

SYN sweeps (many SYN packets, one source, no completed handshakes)
Specific data lengths (40 bytes = stealth scan, 44 = SYN scan, 60 = connect scan)
Flag combinations that no legitimate OS would send (NULL, Xmas, FIN-only) 
It's pure network-layer pattern matching. WAF has no role here — it only sees HTTP. 

Metasploit → depends on the phase:

Phase	What's happening	What catches it
Exploit delivery (e.g., SMB buffer overflow, RDP exploit)	Known exploit payload sent to a network service	NIPS — signature match on the exploit pattern
Web app exploit (e.g., SQLi via Metasploit's sqlmap-style modules)	Malicious payload in an HTTP parameter	WAF — it's parsing the decoded HTTP request
Post-exploitation / C2 (Meterpreter reverse shell, staged payloads)	Encrypted callback to attacker's machine	Neither, really — this is where you need EDR
(Endpoint Detection and Response) or host-based monitoring

That last row is the important one for the exam. Meterpreter specifically uses encrypted communication back to the attacker. 
A network IPS sitting outside the TLS boundary sees ciphertext and is blind. The detection has to happen at the endpoint — looking for unusual process spawns, 
unexpected outbound connections to rare ports, script interpreters launched from unusual parent processes. 

Mutley framing:

Nmap = Mutley walking up to the gate and trying every lock on the fence (the IPS sees him doing it and blocks him).
Metasploit exploit = Mutley picking the lock with a known tool (IPS has the signature for that specific lockpick).
Meterpreter C2 = Mutley already inside the building, calling his buddy outside through an encrypted phone. The gate (IPS) can't hear that. You need a security camera 
inside the room (EDR/host-based) to see that Alan is suddenly talking to a stranger on a burner phone.
So the exam takeaway:
IPS handles the network-level stuff (scans, exploits to services), WAF handles the web-app-level stuff, and endpoint detection handles what happens after the initial 
compromise. They're complementary, not interchangeable

Your SOC team is complaining that they're getting hundreds of alerts a day, most of which are false positives. You suspect the detection method is the problem. 
Which method are you likely running, and what's the trade-off if you switch?

The detection method is anomaly-based, which has a high false-positive rate. Switching to signature-based would reduce noise, but you'd lose visibility into 
unknown/zero-day threats. The best approach is a hybrid with tuned thresholds.
The culprit: Anomaly-based detection. It builds a baseline of "normal" and flags deviations — but "normal" shifts (new software, seasonal traffic spikes, a new department
onboarding), so it generates a lot of false positives. That's the classic reason SOC teams drown in alerts.

The fix (and the trade-off):

Switch to (or weight more heavily) signature-based detection.

Gain: Drastically fewer false positives — it only alerts on known patterns, so it's precise.
Loss: You lose zero-day / novel attack detection. If an attacker uses a technique that's not in the signature database, you're blind.
The real-world answer (and the one the exam accepts): use both. Signatures for the known stuff (low noise), anomaly detection as a secondary layer tuned with a higher 
threshold so it only alerts on significant deviations rather than every small one.

A net work TAP is used to feed traffic to an IDS. What happens to the IDS's ability to act on malicious traffic if you tried to use it as an IPS instead?
An IPS behind a TAP only sees a mirror copy of the traffic — it's not in the data path, so it has no ability to drop packets, reset connections, or block IPs.
It can detect and alert, but it behaves exactly like an IDS in that configuration.
To act as a true IPS, it needs to be inline — physically in the path so traffic flows through it and it can intervene in real time.

If you try to run an Intrusion Prevention System (IPS) behind a standard passive network TAP, it cannot directly block or drop malicious traffic. Because it only receives 
a duplicate copy of the packets rather than sitting directly in the traffic path, it is effectively reduced to functioning as a passive Intrusion Detection System (IDS).

(Scenario) You're designing a network for a hospital. Patient monitoring systems are on the internal LAN. You need visibility into all traffic for compliance logging,
but you cannot introduce any single point of failure that could interrupt patient data flow. Do you deploy an IDS or an IPS at the internal switch, and why?

I would deploy a passive IDS (Intrusion Detection System) connected via a network TAP or a switch SPAN/mirror port. In a clinical environment with life-critical patient
monitoring systems, continuous availability takes priority over automated blocking.

Why IDS is the Right Choice Here

• Eliminates single points of failure. Because an IDS sits out-of-band and only analyzes a copied stream of traffic, a hardware crash, power loss, or software hang on
the sensor has zero effect on the live switch forwarding patient telemetry.
• Prevents false-positive outages. An inline IPS can mistakenly flag legitimate, proprietary medical device traffic (such as HL7 or DICOM streams) as anomalous 
and drop packets, which could disrupt real-time patient alarms or vitals monitoring.
• Satisfies compliance logging. A passive IDS captures full packet headers, metadata, and security alerts for audit trails and HIPAA/compliance logging without adding 
latency to the production LAN.

What's the relationship between a next-gen firewall and an IPS? If you already have an NGFW, do you still need a separate IPS?

For the exam: the key takeaway is that a traditional firewall + separate IPS is the old architecture. An NGFW consolidates that into one appliance. The exam won't ask you
to design a standalone IPS deployment — it'll ask you to recognise that an NGFW includes IPS functionality, and that this reduces appliance sprawl and simplifies policy 
management. 


Mutley, the strict, no-nonsense security dog who takes his job very seriously (whether he's stamping tickets at the Kerberos booth, snitching from the sidelines as an IDS, 
or bouncing intruders at the door as an IPS

(Mutley question 🐕) If Mutley was Kerberos, the strict ticketing dog. Mutley validates tickets with "special ink." If Mutley were an IDS, what would change about his behaviour?
If he were an IPS, what would change?
If Mutley steps down from guarding the Kerberos ticket booth and takes on network security duties, his physical position at the door and his power to stop intruders change 
completely.

Here is how Mutley's behavior shifts depending on his new role:

1. Mutley as an IDS (The Hallway Snitch)

• Where he stands: Mutley is no longer blocking the doorway. He sits off to the side in a booth behind a one-way mirror, watching photocopies of everyone's tickets go by.
• What he does: He still inspects the "special ink" on every ticket copy, looking for forged signatures, expired timestamps, or suspicious behavior.
• How he reacts to a fake: He does not jump up or tackle the intruder. The person with the fake ticket walks right into the building unimpeded. Instead, Mutley writes down the intruder's description in a logbook, blows a whistle, or sends an urgent memo to the security team—and maybe lets out his signature wheezy snicker while watching the chaos unfold.

2. Mutley as an IPS (The Bouncer Dog at the Door)

• Where he stands: Mutley is back directly in the doorway. Nobody gets past him without handing over their ticket first.
• What he does: Just like before, he checks the special ink on every ticket—except now he isn't just checking Kerberos passes; he is also patting people down for hidden 
weapons, known attack patterns, and sketchy behavior.
• How he reacts to a fake: The instant Mutley spots bad ink or a threat, he slams the door shut on the spot, rips (or should I say eats) up the fake ticket, and kicks the
intruder out before they ever step foot inside—before quietly snickering under his breath and waiting for the next person in line.

Quick Comparison

Mutley's Role	         Position	                                                            Reaction to a Forged Ticket
Kerberos	          At the ticket booth	                                      Checks cryptographic "ink" and hands out access passes (or refuses to issue one).
IDS	                Watching from the sidelines	                              Lets the intruder pass, logs the fake ticket, and alerts the admin.
IPS	                Blocking the doorway	                                    Stops the intruder cold, drops their ticket in the trash, and blocks entry.


